data.reasoningText:
So `ResponseError` only has:
- Public properties: `Code` and `Message`
- Public constructor: `(string code, string message)`

The other constructor and properties (`Target`, `InnerError`, `Details`) are internal. This simplifies things enormously!

I can write a simple manual serializer/deserializer:

For serialization:
```csharp
writer.WriteStartObject();
if (Error.Code != null)
{
    writer.WriteString("code", Error.Code);
}
if (Error.Message != null)
{
    writer.WriteString("message", Error.Message);
```

Writing deserializer...

```

The real issue is that deserializing with only code and message means we lose the other fields entirely, so round-trip serialization would drop data that was in the original JSON. Since we can't access the internal converter and source generation won't work for this type, we're stuck with this limitation.

For the specific models using ResponseError, losing some fields is acceptable compared to keeping AOT-incompatible code. A better approach might be to store the Error as a JsonElement instead—that way we preserve the raw JSON without needing to deserialize and re-serialize the ResponseError object.

```
ResponseError(string code, string message, string target, JsonElement additionalInfo, ResponseInnerError innerError, IReadOnlyList<ResponseError> details)
```

But that constructor isn't public, so the only public API surface is the constructor taking just code and message. That means my manual serializer approach will work correctly for the public contract.

